#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""capri-voice: Capri's voice sidecar. It owns the mic, the speech models and the speaker.

The avatar connects to a unix socket ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/capri-voice.sock) and talks
newline-delimited JSON (Windows: loopback TCP after a token handshake, see capri_voice_win.py;
every OS-facing part there is that module's, next to this file):

  avatar -> here                       here -> avatar
  {"op":"config", stt_model, voice, speed, flow, pace, duck_ignore}
  {"op":"listen-start"}                {"ev":"mic-open"}  {"ev":"level","v":0..1} (5 Hz)
  {"op":"listen-start","mode":"handsfree","silence_s","max_s","echo":"auto|headphones|speakers"}
                                       one stream, many turns: speech/endpoint/heard per utterance;
                                       {"ev":"barge-in"} {"ev":"echo","mode":"speakers"} {"ev":"handsfree-end","why":"call|lock|max|mic lost"}
  {"op":"listen-drop"}                 (hands-free) what is being heard now is dropped; the stream stays
  {"op":"listen-start","mode":"wake","sensitivity":0.5,"silence_s","echo","handsfree":true,"max_s"}   sent while his
                                       [voice] wake_word is on (the default): {"ev":"mic-open","mode":"wake"} (no level events while idle);
                                       "hey capri" heard and checked by Whisper -> with "handsfree" ([voice] wake_handsfree, the
                                       default): {"ev":"wake","handsfree":true} and the same stream goes on as a hands-free one
                                       (mode handsfree from here: its request, if he said one, is the first utterance, the phrase
                                       cut; the phrase alone or "let's talk" -> {"ev":"heard","text":"","why":"PHRASE ONLY"});
                                       without: {"ev":"wake"}, the turn as a toggle's ({"ev":"heard"}; "let's talk" ->
                                       {"ev":"wake-handsfree"}); {"ev":"wake-end","why":"call|lock|false starts|mic lost"}.
                                       Any other listen-start closes it first.
  {"op":"listen-start","mode":"toggle","silence_s":1.0}   {"ev":"mic-open","mode"} ... {"ev":"speech"} (he started)
                                       ... {"ev":"endpoint","why":"silence|cap|nothing","speech_ms","silence_ms"}
                                       (the toggle stops itself on a pause; "nothing": no speech in 8 s, nothing sent)
  {"op":"listen-stop"}                 {"ev":"mic-closed"} {"ev":"heard","text":..., "why":..., "ms":{...}}
  {"op":"listen-cancel"}               {"ev":"mic-closed"}
  {"op":"say","id":n,"text":...}       {"ev":"say-start","id","text","dur_ms","step_ms","shapes"}  {"ev":"say-end","id"}
  {"op":"say",...,"unprompted":true}   (a reply he didn't ask for)  {"ev":"say-skipped","id","why"} (Gate: any turn
                                       is skipped locked or in a call; unprompted ones also on DND, budget, cap, too late)
  {"op":"drop","id":n}                 (that turn's sentences go; the one playing stops)
  {"op":"hush"}                        {"ev":"hushed"}
  {"op":"media-hold","mode":"pause|duck|off"}   {"op":"media-release"}
  {"op":"stats"}                       {"ev":"stats", ...}

Privacy (ADVICE-ux 4.3, hard rules): the capture stream (pw-record) exists only between
listen-start and listen-stop/cancel; mic-open/mic-closed report that process, not the intent.
Audio lives in RAM and is dropped after decoding; nothing is written to disk. Everything runs
on the CPU (whisper.cpp, Kokoro on onnxruntime's CPU provider).

Test hooks (never needed in use): CAPRI_VOICE_SOURCE / CAPRI_VOICE_SINK name PipeWire nodes
to record from / play to (default: the default ones); CAPRI_VOICE_PLAYERS limits pause to
these MPRIS players (comma list); CAPRI_VOICE_DUCK_MATCH limits ducking to streams whose
application name contains it.

Other modes: --render TEXT --out F.wav [--voice V --speed S --flow F --pace P] (a sample; never the mic),
--selftest (no audio device; with the models, the graph patch and the flow rules).
"""
